WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.378 --> 00:00:11.090
Your first job as a speaker is to build
a trusting human bond

00:00:11.090 --> 00:00:12.425
with your audience.

00:00:12.550 --> 00:00:15.970
One excellent way to connect
to an audience is to tell a story.

00:00:16.387 --> 00:00:19.223
Stories helped make us who we are.

00:00:19.766 --> 00:00:23.728
Really — evidence left
behind from our ancient ancestors

00:00:23.811 --> 00:00:29.442
suggests that the human mind
developed alongside human storytelling.

00:00:29.817 --> 00:00:33.029
Fire created a new magnet for bonding.

00:00:33.279 --> 00:00:37.450
Its warmth and flickering light
drew people together after dark.

00:00:38.034 --> 00:00:41.162
This seems to have happened
in every ancient culture

00:00:41.162 --> 00:00:44.040
over the last 300,000 years.

00:00:44.248 --> 00:00:47.251
And what did our ancestors do
with this time together?

00:00:47.460 --> 00:00:48.878
In many cultures,

00:00:48.878 --> 00:00:54.050
one type of communal activity became
very important: storytelling.

00:00:54.342 --> 00:00:58.513
Stories brought people from
faraway places to the fireside.

00:00:58.679 --> 00:01:02.058
Stories about people who were alive
and people who were dead,

00:01:02.391 --> 00:01:04.811
nearby and in distant lands.

00:01:05.228 --> 00:01:08.856
Stories sparked laughter,
tension, and amazement.

00:01:09.482 --> 00:01:14.612
Stories also played an important role
in helping expand people’s ability

00:01:14.612 --> 00:01:20.243
to imagine and to dream,
and to understand the minds of others.

00:01:20.743 --> 00:01:23.913
Storytellers became
important people within a community,

00:01:23.913 --> 00:01:26.624
and their listeners took information
from the stories

00:01:26.624 --> 00:01:29.293
that changed how they behaved
in the world.

00:01:29.669 --> 00:01:32.713
It's not just that we all
love hearing stories.

00:01:33.172 --> 00:01:39.011
Stories probably helped shape how
our minds share and receive information.

00:01:39.470 --> 00:01:44.559
So it's no surprise that many of the best
talks are built around a story.

00:01:46.060 --> 00:01:49.605
The most basic way of breaking
down a story is this:

00:01:50.106 --> 00:01:53.484
a character with a goal
meets an unexpected obstacle.

00:01:53.985 --> 00:01:58.364
She tries to overcome that obstacle,
which builds to a peak in the action.

00:01:58.573 --> 00:02:01.701
Finally, there’s some kind
of resolution to the conflict.

00:02:02.368 --> 00:02:05.454
When it comes to sharing
a story from the stage,

00:02:05.663 --> 00:02:08.166
remember four key things:

00:02:08.457 --> 00:02:13.713
1. Base the story around a character
your audience can empathize with.

00:02:14.172 --> 00:02:16.507
2. Build tension,

00:02:16.716 --> 00:02:21.971
either through curiosity, relationship
with other characters, or actual danger.

00:02:22.513 --> 00:02:26.184
3. Offer the right level of detail.

00:02:26.767 --> 00:02:29.437
If there's not enough,
the story won't come to life.

00:02:29.520 --> 00:02:32.190
If there's too much,
it will get bogged down.

00:02:32.857 --> 00:02:39.822
And, 4. End with a satisfying conclusion,
whether it’s funny, moving, or surprising.

00:02:40.615 --> 00:02:44.869
Some great talks are built
around a single story.

00:02:45.328 --> 00:02:47.455
This can be really helpful for a speaker.

00:02:47.622 --> 00:02:50.333
The throughline is simply
the plot of the story.

00:02:50.875 --> 00:02:55.004
If the story is about you, it can even
create empathy in the audience.

00:02:55.588 --> 00:02:58.216
If your journey is exciting
and easy to follow,

00:02:58.466 --> 00:03:01.177
this type of talk can work really well.

00:03:01.427 --> 00:03:03.054
But there's a trap here too.

00:03:04.055 --> 00:03:06.807
Remember, the goal is to give.

00:03:07.225 --> 00:03:10.937
Personal stories may entertain
or interest an audience,

00:03:11.145 --> 00:03:14.440
or make the speaker
feel really good about themselves,

00:03:14.565 --> 00:03:17.485
but they don’t necessarily
give the audience

00:03:17.485 --> 00:03:19.278
something they can walk away with;

00:03:19.320 --> 00:03:24.325
a new way of understanding things,
actionable information, context, hope.

00:03:24.492 --> 00:03:26.160
And that’s a real shame.

00:03:26.619 --> 00:03:31.040
The key is to link together
the important moments of your story

00:03:31.040 --> 00:03:34.335
in a way that can be meaningful
to someone else.

00:03:34.794 --> 00:03:37.463
Otherwise, it may sound
like you're just rambling

00:03:37.755 --> 00:03:40.508
or speaking for the sake
of hearing yourself talk.

00:03:41.008 --> 00:03:45.554
But, if the journey reveals something
powerful you have learned,

00:03:45.638 --> 00:03:50.268
and if each step in your journey is
revealed with humility and honesty

00:03:50.434 --> 00:03:51.978
and vulnerability,

00:03:52.061 --> 00:03:54.855
it is a journey
we will gladly take with you.

00:03:55.815 --> 00:03:59.568
There is one other piece
that is absolutely essential

00:03:59.694 --> 00:04:01.988
if you’re going to tell your own story:

00:04:02.530 --> 00:04:04.115
it has to be true.

00:04:04.949 --> 00:04:06.284
This may seem obvious,

00:04:06.534 --> 00:04:09.537
but sometimes speakers are
tempted to exaggerate

00:04:09.787 --> 00:04:12.081
or even make a story up entirely.

00:04:12.540 --> 00:04:15.960
Stories can have such a big impact
that some speakers

00:04:15.960 --> 00:04:17.920
are tempted to bend the truth.

00:04:18.296 --> 00:04:23.134
But even small exaggerations can quickly
destroy your reputation.

00:04:23.551 --> 00:04:25.553
It's not worth the risk.

00:04:26.345 --> 00:04:30.141
When you combine a truthful story
with a desire to benefit others,

00:04:30.433 --> 00:04:33.602
you can give your listeners
an extraordinary gift.

00:04:34.020 --> 00:04:36.355
A story can affect people deeply.

00:04:36.480 --> 00:04:40.026
Use it, and you can greatly increase
your connection with an audience.

00:04:40.776 --> 00:04:43.571
But please, let it mean something.
